 polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1045 "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5612666d-368d-549d-8c98-b899d091fd25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1080 "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04d839b3-7560-5512-ab94-65b25afb8af3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1080 "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" #edb/r